speechtext:
So far as insurance is concerned I have no objection at all to the provision made in that regard. That meant something; that is something within our jurisdiction. My hon. friend says this legislation did not cost the farmer one cent;
I showed the other day that it had cost two and a half millions. I think it probably cost more than that, but I like to be within the mark in these matters; I know it is at least that. We all know that the prices the farmer gets are based on Fort William and Port Arthur and that the furtherance costs from Fort William and Port Arthur take account of the difference in cost between those points and Liverpool. Is there any dispute about that? Part of that cost, the cost based on Fort William and Port Arthur plus the cost o: transportation to Liverpool, is the lake rate. I have already pointed out that in connection with the Buffalo movement, which is a very large movement and on which prices are frequently based, there was in September a range of from 2 to 2f cents before to an average as given by my hon. friend of 4.90 cents and a price of 5 cents, as quoted by me, on September 20th. In October the range of prices from Fort William had been advanced from 5 to 5| and 6J cents as against 3 from Duluth.
[Mr. Sales.!
